    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d513-a1">

                <pb facs="0488" n="390" sameAs="frus:frus1900/pg390"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Squiers to Mr. Wilcox.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Pekin China</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1899-08-25"><hi rendition="#italic">August 25,
                        1899</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">Con., No. 428.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The legation begs to return to you the
                    passport application of Logan H. Roots, which accompanied your dispatch, No. 47,
                    of the 9th instant, for the reason that the applicant in his affidavit states
                    that he expects to reside permanently in China.</p>
                <pb facs="0489" n="391" xml:id="pg391"/>
                <p>Under instructions from the State Department a passport can not issue to a
                    citizen of the United States who has no intention of returning there to reside,
                    but who intends to live permanently abroad. While very liberal in its
                    requirements toward those engaged in missionary work as to <hi
                        rendition="#italic">when</hi> they expect to return, a definite intention to
                    do so at some future time is always required.</p>
                <p>As the applicant is a young man and will probably return many times to his home
                    in the United States, although he may remain permanently engaged in missionary
                    work in China, the application is returned to him for his further consideration
                    and, if possible, a modification of his statement. In case he declines to do so,
                    the application will be referred to the State Department for its
                    consideration.</p>
                <p>It is also noted that you failed to affix your consular seal to the
                    affidavit.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">H. G. Squiers</hi>,<lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Secretary of Legation.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
